<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
  This survey's decision item (a not-yet-landed, hash-keyed item at prepare time — see this topic's own
  <code>relatedReport</code> for its identity) asks whether a dispatched background agent's own transcript
  should be reviewed for <em>how it ran</em>, and scored. The operator's framing is a <strong>benchmark, not a
  checklist</strong>: every dispatched run starts at an implicit 100%, and the pass's job is to identify what
  prevented <em>this specific</em> execution from scoring it — hesitation and waffling before acting, redundant
  or inefficient commands, bad code produced, test failures tailed off rather than addressed. Findings then
  split by risk: low-risk ones auto-apply, larger ones are filed for human review.
</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — the gap is real, and it is precisely the run <em>path</em></h2>
<p>
  Five adjacent mechanisms exist and none of them is this. <code>we:skills-src/inspect-agent-health/agent-health.mjs</code>
  classifies <em>liveness</em> only (<code>ACTIVE</code> / <code>BLOCKED_ON_CHILD</code> /
  <code>BLOCKED_ON_TOOL</code> / <code>IDLE_OR_STALLED</code>). <code>we:scripts/conveyor/hiccup-classify.mjs</code>
  classifies <em>blocking delivery hiccups</em> off tick state. <code>we:scripts/operations/review-pr.mjs</code>
  reviews the <em>diff</em> — its op declaration takes exactly <code>pr</code> and <code>repo</code>, and its
  judge shape asks for "one sentence on the diff as a whole". The learnings pool collects whatever a session
  chose to <em>self-report</em>. The driver watchdog watches the <em>driver process</em>. So a run can be
  <code>ACTIVE</code> throughout, produce zero hiccups, and land a green PR while having burned an hour
  re-reading one file and silently dropping a failing spec — and every existing guard passes it.
</p>

<h2>Finding 2 — the field already names the mechanism: trajectory evaluation</h2>
<p>
  The 2026 agent-evaluation literature calls scoring the <em>path</em> (tool-call sequence, inputs and outputs,
  intermediate reasoning, retries) rather than the final answer <strong>trajectory evaluation</strong>, and names
  the operator's criteria independently: <em>Redundant Tool Calls</em>, <em>Tool Frequency</em>,
  <em>Task Relevance</em>, <em>Execution Efficiency</em>. It states the case in the same terms the operator did —
  an agent that reaches the right answer through a wildly inefficient path "still represents a production
  failure", which final-answer evaluation structurally cannot see. That is independent corroboration that
  reviewing the diff can never substitute for reviewing the run.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>But the standard form differs in one load-bearing way.</strong> Published trajectory evaluations get
  their comparability from a <strong>fixed task set</strong>. Here every run is a different backlog item of a
  different size, dispatched to a different model at a different effort tier. So this is <em>not</em> a benchmark
  in the SWE-bench / MCP-Bench sense, and the design must not silently inherit their comparability.
</p>

<h2>Finding 3 — three mature deduction-scoring systems, and each settles one of the operator's sub-questions</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Lighthouse</strong> — the 0–100 Performance score is a <em>weighted average of per-metric scores</em>:
    the per-metric numbers are the record, the headline is derived. Weights have changed across major versions,
    and the project's own guidance is that a cross-version comparison is meaningless, because a score can move
    because the algorithm changed rather than the subject. History is <strong>never re-normalised</strong>. This
    settles two questions at once: the deduction vector is canonical and the scalar is derived (a scalar cannot
    be decomposed back into a vector), and a rubric change mints a new version rather than rewriting the past.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>FOQA</strong> (aviation Flight Operational Quality Assurance) — every flight is scored against
    parameter thresholds by <em>exceedance detection</em>. Thresholds sit in a zone <em>approaching</em> a limit,
    so routine flights register exceedances as a matter of course; and decisively, <strong>a singular exceedance
    may not trigger corrective action at all</strong> — mitigation follows the <em>aggregate</em> exceedance rate
    crossing a predetermined threshold. This is the mature answer to "is 100% realistic": a scoring system over
    real operations that treats a perfect score as neither expected nor the trigger for action.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Chess engine accuracy</strong> — every move is graded against the engine's best and averaged into a
    0–100 accuracy with named bands. <strong>90–100% is grandmaster-level, and grandmasters routinely score
    below 100.</strong> A system that flagged every inaccuracy as actionable would be unusable.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Finding 4 — the risk dial and its approval gate are already ratified in this repo</h2>
<p>
  <code>assessMissingOperationConfidence</code> (<code>we:scripts/conveyor/hiccup-classify.mjs</code>) already
  returns <code>{selfClears, batched, escalate}</code>: an operation blacklist is checked first and independently
  (→ <code>escalate</code>), any flagged confidence criterion (<code>securityRisk</code>,
  <code>dataLeakRisk</code>, <code>performance</code>, <code>blastRadius</code>,
  <code>baselineCorrectness</code>) yields <code>batched</code>, and a clean call <code>selfClears</code>. That
  <em>is</em> "low-risk auto-applies, higher-risk gets filed". The transport is equally reusable:
  <code>fileHiccup</code> (<code>we:scripts/conveyor/hiccup-sink.mjs</code>) writes an
  <code>approvalPending</code> entry into the same pool rather than a parallel store,
  <code>we:scripts/conveyor/hiccup-approve.mjs</code> clears it, and one line of
  <code>we:scripts/conveyor/learnings-harvest.mjs</code> holds a gated entry out of clustering entirely.
</p>
<p>
  What does <strong>not</strong> transfer is the <em>axis</em> that decision #3422 ratified alongside it. Its axis
  is "did the tick get blocked", and a run-quality finding on a <em>successful</em> run is non-blocking by that
  definition — which #3422 rules goes "file the card only, no fix proposed, straight through with no gate". That
  is the opposite of auto-applying a fix. Reuse the mechanism; reject the axis.
</p>

<h2>Finding 5 — the learnings pool cannot hold a scorecard, so the score needs its own store</h2>
<p>
  <code>we:scripts/conveyor/learnings-drop.mjs</code>'s <code>ALLOWED_KEYS</code> is an allow-list enforced by
  <code>validateEntry</code> — an un-allow-listed key is <strong>rejected</strong>, not dropped — and
  <code>FIELD_CAPS</code> caps <code>summary</code> at 240 characters and <code>suggestion</code> at 400. A
  per-criterion deduction vector fits neither. Findings can route through the pool; the score cannot live there.
</p>
<p>
  The store has an exact in-repo precedent. <code>we:scripts/check-app-conformance.mjs</code> computes a rounded
  percentage and <strong>defaults to 100 when nothing was measured</strong> — structurally the same
  "starts at 100%" premise — then appends one row per run to a burndown JSON and reads the series back. The
  alternative hanger, <code>we:scripts/operations/run-record.mjs</code>, is per-run and already carries
  <code>costUsd</code>, <code>durationMs</code>, <code>numTurns</code> and <code>loadedContextTokens</code>, but
  its telemetry allow-list forbids a drive-by field and
  <code>we:scripts/operations/step-timings-report.mjs</code> states the standing posture directly: a distribution
  or trend over those rows is "a separate card, not a reason to grow this one".
</p>

<h2>Finding 6 — the subject might itself be a live driver, and that gates everything before risk does</h2>
<p>
  A conveyor runner or driver is itself a transcript-producing process — one that holds a queue, dispatches other
  work, and is <em>still running</em> while its transcript is read. Auto-applying a fix to or around such a
  process on an external judge's say-so is a second, less careful path to precisely the danger the existing
  watchdog and validate-and-promote design was built to prevent: that design records last-known-good rather than
  inferring it, requires a long quiet period <em>and</em> no in-flight work <em>and</em> a live lease before it
  acts, and heals by recorded rollback plus an existing restart operation.
</p>
<p>
  The repo has already ruled the neighbouring case. Decision #2077's rider, codified under
  <code>#pr-flow-rollout-mechanism</code>, states the invariant that a run's own executing tooling is never
  modified in the checkout a live run executes it from, and cites uniform external prior art: GitHub Actions pins
  a run's workflow definition at run start, Jenkins pins shared-library versions per run, and merge bots run from
  a deployment outside the tree they land. Tooling changes ride the normal queue and take effect on the
  <em>next</em> run. The two rules key on different tests — #2077 on the edit's <em>touch-set</em>, this one on
  the <em>subject class</em> that produced the transcript — so they compose rather than duplicate.
</p>

<h2>Finding 7 — the shared-code-is-shared-failure rule is written down and machine-asserted</h2>
<p>
  The driver watchdog's own header states the rule it exists to obey: it must not share the driver's own decision
  logic, because "a watchdog that asked the driver's own planner 'is there eligible work?' would answer 'no' for
  precisely the same wrong reason the driver did — shared code is shared failure." Its test suite asserts the
  import graph with an exact-equality assertion rather than a negative one, so the allow-list cannot be widened
  silently. The technique is a house pattern in this repo, not a one-off. A run-quality scorer inherits the same
  reflexivity — a dispatched run's quality is partly a property of the dispatcher — so the reader and scorer
  should obey the rule while the router, which merely files and applies, need not.
</p>

<h2>Finding 8 — the scope is smaller than the original framing assumed</h2>
<p>
  Decision #3475 (resolved, codified at <code>#automated-session-introspection</code>) already ratifies a detached
  judge pass over a real transcript on every terminating session, at three triggers, emitting into the existing
  learnings pool. A dispatched lane agent is a top-level session rather than an Agent-tool subagent, so of those
  triggers the reaper and <code>SessionEnd</code> reach it. <strong>That is the reader.</strong> What a
  criteria-expansion of #3475 cannot give you is a score, a rubric version, a trend, a subject-class gate, or an
  auto-apply path — because #3475 ratified a destination whose schema structurally excludes all five. So the
  honest scope is a rubric, a scorecard store, a subject-class gate and a risk router layered onto an existing
  reader, not a new mechanism.
</p>
<p>
  One concrete gap must be closed by whatever is built: no dispatch record stores a transcript path. What is
  stored is the dispatcher-minted session handle, from which the transcript reconstructs as a
  <em>session-level</em> file — and the existing transcript resolver probes only the nested subagents directory,
  so it would miss a dispatched lane agent today.
</p>

<h2>Finding 9 — the criteria do not need inventing</h2>
<p>
  They can be derived from what this repo has already written down: the twelve rules of its mechanical-delivery
  doctrine (a bespoke prompt where a declared operation exists; a concluded external limitation accepted without
  checking; symptom relief mistaken for a fix; hand-dispatching what the conveyor already covers), the pinned
  rule against ending a turn on a passive wait, the two syntactic transcript patterns already specified for a
  fleet scanner, and 267 durable agent-memory notes. The <em>form</em> has a precedent too:
  <code>we:scripts/lib/review-core.mjs</code> holds a frozen one-sentence-per-lens expectation map where "the
  wording IS the commitment", alongside a concrete "go looking for these shapes, in roughly this order of past
  frequency" hunt brief. And <code>summarizeEntry</code> in the agent-health tool already decomposes one
  transcript line into text, thinking, tool-use and tool-result blocks — which is hesitation, redundant commands
  and ignored failures, already parsed.
</p>

<h2>Naming</h2>
<p>
  "Observer" is already taken three times over in this repo, all for polling <em>dispatch state</em>. The concept
  surveyed here is therefore named the <strong>run-quality auditor</strong>, producing a <strong>run
  scorecard</strong> — reusing "scorecard" deliberately, since an existing grammar scorecard is the same noun for
  the same thing.
</p>
